 {"id": "MON-REV19-11","type": null,"capability_tags": ["+monitoring","alert-router","api","observability"],"reasoning": "Measured: POST /webhook with lane='NOT A LANE' -> HTTP 200, rejected_total 7->8, no row, log line\n'rejecting alert with unusable lane label'. Alertmanager therefore treats a refused alert as delivered\n(its retry logic never fires). Same shape for the pre-resolve path: the only signal is a counter name\nand a log line quoting a -RESOLVED id, which is easy to read as 'the alert cleared'.\nAlso measured: a webhook with an empty alerts list bumps received_total by 1 ('len(alerts) or 1'), so the\ncounter over-reports delivery on empty batches.\nACCEPTANCE: refusals return a non-2xx (or a documented 2xx + machine-readable refusal), and counters\nseparate refused / no-such-series / cleared; empty batches do not increment received_total.\nEstimate ~30 min. Evidence: ~/project-reviews/reviews/monitoring/2026-10-19-carter.html","priority": "P3","complexity": 2,"created_at": "2026-10-20T01:33:32.372752+00:00","updated_at": "2026-10-20T01:33:32.372752+00:00","title": "P3 — router answers 200 for alerts it refuses (unusable lane, rejected auth aside) and exposes no distinct counter for 'no such series' vs 'cleared', so callers cannot tell","status": "pending","detail": {"fingerprint": "f443d6125fe0949bc9f715bce4c3cf4f38855e38a4650d60b9b2b0e27c8968fe"},"acceptance": "grep -n 'rejecting' stack/alert-router/router.py must print the unknown-lane reject log line (today line 417) and it must sit in a branch that only bumps rejected_total and logs; done = that branch also returns a 4xx status. | curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' -X POST -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{\\\"lane\\\":\\\"NOT A LANE\\\"}' http://127.0.0.1:8089/webhook must print 200 today (rejected_total increments, no row written); after the fix it must print a 4xx status code."}
 {"id": "MON-MONITORING-TICK-20261020-0244","type": "note","lane": "monitoring","status": "complete","priority": "P2","title": "monitoring tick 2026-10-20-02-44: drain 0-appended/705-dedup/0-unwritable; InventorySweepStale root-caused (420s tool-cap swallowed the publish) and FIXED live; TargetUnreachable verified vanished-host + stale probe target named","reasoning": "TICK 2026-10-20 02:44 (-05). DRAIN: MON_REMOTE=fleet-monitor drain-board-rows.sh -> appended=0 skipped=705 unwritable=0. Verified rather than assumed: parsed all 705 spool rows (all lane=monitoring, 0 unparsable, 0 missing id) and replicated the drain's dedupe per row -> 705 already-on-board, 0 lanes without a board, 0 rows missing. The spool is an accumulating log that is never cleared remotely (clearing is deliberately manual), so a large skipped count is the expected green result (pitfall G), not a drain failure. LIVE FIRING SET (fleet-monitor:9090 /api/v1/alerts, 02:47Z): TargetUnreachable x2 (standby-cx53-01-2147 ports 10001/10002, critical) + InventorySweepStale (fleet-monitor, warning). TRIAGE 1 - InventorySweepStale: TRUE POSITIVE, ROOT-CAUSED AND FIXED. The weekly hermes cron job fleet-inventory-sweep (6bebf202795d, '0 4 * * 1') runs, but its 2026-10-19 fire launched inventory-sweep.sh in the FOREGROUND and hit the terminal tool cap (agent.log.2 'sequential tool terminal timed out after 420.0s' at 04:07:20), then ended last_status=ok at 04:18:33 WITHOUT publishing: fleet_inventory.prom on fleet-monitor was frozen at 2026-10-12 09:00 (stamp 1791795652, age 7.75d) with no 2026-10-19 snapshot dir. FIX: re-ran the producer as a TRACKED BACKGROUND process -> exit 0, 'published: inventoried=12 containers=178 not_reporting=6 unreachable=5'; host file mtime 2026-10-20 02:53, stamp 1792464825, new snapshot /root/fleet-monitoring/inventory/2026-10-20. ALERT CLEARED, proved through the metric layer: time()-fleet_inventory_last_success_timestamp_seconds = 155.5s and InventorySweepStale is absent from /ap